Groupon marketing roles were 70% Product Manager roles. This is because the role included taking care and managing the in-house Martech. Our internal infrastructure - called “Flywheel” -  would generate - based on templates - and publish to Google Ads account: campaigns, ad groups, creatives, keywords. Flywheel was connected to MDS the internal database and api at Groupon where every single piece of information about a deal was contained and it fuelled every single marketing channel and Groupon website. If deal was sold out, the info would go from MDS to Flywheel and the campaigns would be stopped. Same for the website where you would read "Sold out”.   


This system - called Flywheel - would also pause ad groups based on deal availability from Groupon Marketing Intelligence database (called “MDS”). Keyword generation was also part of the job and automated. 


The system would use as keywords: the merchant name (from database), keyword who drove traffic to the merchant’s website (based on SEM Rush api), Keyword tool, and Smart keywords. Filters were in place to prevent bad keywords from being added (example of filtering: search volume from Google Keyword planner, keyword denylist internally created).    


When Covid hitted, Groupon went through tough times and people cut. This meant Flywheel was dismantled. As Senior Manager for global SEM I worked on finding an out of the shelf alternative. My Director and I chose Search Ads 360 and I led both my SEM team and the remaining Computational Marketing engineering team in the transition to SA360. This meant the onboarding to the tool, build the feed for the templates, floodlight installation, Smart bidding testing and enablement, and offline conversion upload (to upload our internal data for conversion since included the revenue per click of each keyword based on Groupon margins)   
